 * `enable` warns when a hand-written watch (`ameesh-veille-<A>.timer`) is still
   installed: disable it (`systemctl --user disable --now …`) and remove its files.
+* A hand-written unit (a watch, a merge watcher, a follow-up script) does not
+  get the login `PATH`: `~/.local/bin` is absent, so a bare `ameesh …` dies
+  with `Errno 2` and the wake-up it carried is lost. Call the account's
+  `ameesh` by absolute path (`~` is not expanded either:
+  `/home/<user>/.local/bin/ameesh`), or pin it in the unit
+  (`Environment=PATH=/home/<user>/.local/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin`).
+  A hand-written unit carries no agent identity either: `mail send` and
+  `work note` fail with `unbound sender` unless `AGENT_MAIL_NAME=<agent>` is
+  exported. Prefer `ameesh watch enable` when a plain periodic watch fits.
+* A unit written by hand must be **enabled from a file** to survive a reboot: a
+  `systemd-run --user --unit=…` instance is transient (it lives under
+  `/run/user/<uid>/systemd/transient/`), so the next boot forgets the watch and
+  the wake-up it carried is lost without a trace. Write
+  `~/.config/systemd/user/<name>.service`, enable it
+  (`systemctl --user enable --now <name>`, which creates the
+  `default.target.wants/` symlink) and check what is loaded with
+  `systemctl --user show <name> -p FragmentPath --value`;
+  `ameesh watch enable` already writes durable units.
 
